<item>
  <title>Getting JSON out of contracts is easy. Proving it’s right is the project.</title>
  <dc:creator>Santosh Kumar Panda</dc:creator>
  <link>https://mediansant.github.io/myblog/posts/doc-intel-contract-extraction/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<div class="callout callout-style-simple callout-note">
<div class="callout-body d-flex">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container">
<p><strong>TL;DR.</strong> <code>doc-intel</code> turns contract PDFs into typed records (governing law, parties, dates, key clauses) and scores every field against the <a href="https://www.atticusprojectai.org/cuad">CUAD</a> expert labels. Governing law and agreement date land at about 0.9+. Liability caps and non-competes are <em>precise but badly under-recalled</em>, which is exactly the kind of thing you only learn by measuring. Code: <a href="https://github.com/mediansant/doc-intel">github.com/mediansant/doc-intel</a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<section id="the-problem-is-not-extraction" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-problem-is-not-extraction">The problem is not extraction</h2>
<p>Anyone can ask an LLM for JSON. The hard question is the one that comes after: <strong>is the JSON right?</strong></p>
<p>So this project is built around that question rather than around the extraction itself:</p>
<table class="caption-top table">
<colgroup>
<col style="width: 50%">
<col style="width: 50%">
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Principle</th>
<th>How it shows up</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td><strong>Typed output</strong></td>
<td>Every field lives in a Pydantic schema, and each value carries a verbatim <em>evidence quote</em> a reviewer can check against the contract.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><strong>Ground truth</strong></td>
<td>Results are scored against 510 CUAD contracts, per field, as precision and recall.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><strong>Reproducible runs</strong></td>
<td>Each run is stored with its model, prompt hash and schema version, so “did prompt v2 beat v1?” is a SQL query, not a guess.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><strong>Cheap to change</strong></td>
<td>Extraction is exposed as an API with caching. The eval harness runs offline against saved outputs.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section id="what-gets-extracted" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="what-gets-extracted">What gets extracted</h2>
<table class="caption-top table">
<colgroup>
<col style="width: 33%">
<col style="width: 33%">
<col style="width: 33%">
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Field</th>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td><code>governing_law</code></td>
<td>text</td>
<td>Jurisdiction as a bare place name, e.g.&nbsp;<code>Delaware</code></td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><code>parties</code></td>
<td>list</td>
<td>Contracting entities named in the preamble</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><code>agreement_date</code></td>
<td>ISO date</td>
<td>Date the contract was entered into</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><code>renewal_term</code></td>
<td>free text</td>
<td>Renewal / extension provisions</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><code>cap_on_liability</code></td>
<td>bool</td>
<td>Explicit monetary cap on liability</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><code>non_compete</code></td>
<td>bool</td>
<td>Non-compete clause present</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section id="the-pipeline" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-pipeline">The pipeline</h2>
<div class="cell" data-layout-align="default">
<div class="cell-output-display">
<div>
<p></p><figure class="figure"><p></p>
<div>
<pre class="mermaid mermaid-js">flowchart TD
    A[("CUAD PDFs +&lt;br/&gt;master_clauses.csv")] --&gt;|"parse.py · Docling"| B["Markdown"]
    B --&gt;|"extract.py · OpenAI structured output"| C["ContractRecord&lt;br/&gt;values + evidence"]
    C --&gt;|"backfill.py"| D[("DuckDB&lt;br/&gt;documents · runs · extractions")]
    D --&gt;|"score.py + matchers.py"| E["Precision / recall&lt;br/&gt;per field + failure list"]
    A -.-&gt;|"ground-truth labels"| E
</pre>
</div>
<p></p></figure><p></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>DuckDB holds the whole story, and it is append-only, so no run ever overwrites another:</p>
<table class="caption-top table">
<colgroup>
<col style="width: 50%">
<col style="width: 50%">
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Table</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td><code>documents</code></td>
<td>One row per PDF, keyed by the SHA-256 of its bytes</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><code>runs</code></td>
<td>One row per (model, prompt hash, schema version)</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><code>extractions</code></td>
<td>The full record as JSON, plus tokens and latency</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><code>eval_outcomes</code></td>
<td>One TP / FP / FN / TN row per field per document per run</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><code>contract_records</code></td>
<td>A view that types the JSON into columns for SQL analytics</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>
<section id="serving-it-post-extract" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="serving-it-post-extract">Serving it: <code>POST /extract</code></h2>
<p>The same extraction is available as a FastAPI service. Results are cached by PDF hash + model + prompt + schema, so uploading the same file twice only costs one model call.</p>
<div class="cell" data-layout-align="default">
<div class="cell-output-display">
<div>
<p></p><figure class="figure"><p></p>
<div>
<pre class="mermaid mermaid-js">sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Client
    participant API as FastAPI
    participant DB as DuckDB
    participant Doc as Docling
    participant LLM as OpenAI

    Client-&gt;&gt;API: POST /extract (PDF)
    API-&gt;&gt;API: doc_id = sha256(bytes)
    API-&gt;&gt;DB: already extracted?
    alt cache hit
        DB--&gt;&gt;API: stored record
        API--&gt;&gt;Client: 200 (cached: true)
    else cache miss
        API-&gt;&gt;Doc: PDF to Markdown
        API-&gt;&gt;LLM: Markdown + schema
        LLM--&gt;&gt;API: ContractRecord
        API-&gt;&gt;DB: insert document + extraction
        API--&gt;&gt;Client: 200 (cached: false)
    end
</pre>
</div>
<p></p></figure><p></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Failure modes are explicit: <strong>413</strong> over 50 MB, <strong>415</strong> not a PDF, <strong>422</strong> unparseable or no text, <strong>502</strong> model error. A 502 is deliberately <em>not</em> cached, so a retry works.</p>
</section>
<section id="how-scoring-works" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="how-scoring-works">How scoring works</h2>
<p>Comparing an LLM’s answer to a human label is where most evals quietly go wrong. Each field goes through three steps:</p>
<ol type="1">
<li><strong>Normalise both sides.</strong> <code>State of Delaware</code> and <code>Delaware</code> match. <code>England and Wales</code> becomes <code>{england, wales}</code>. Party aliases like <code>("Chase")</code> are dropped.</li>
<li><strong>Decide absence with one rule</strong> for every field type (<code>None</code>, <code>False</code>, empty, <code>NaN</code>).</li>
<li><strong>Classify</strong> as TP / FP / FN / TN. A wrong value counts as <em>both</em> a false positive and a false negative.</li>
</ol>
<p>Two guard rails I’d keep in any eval harness: the scorer asserts that outcomes cover every scored contract, and it <strong>lists skipped files instead of hiding them</strong>. Partial dates like <code>03/[]/1999</code> never match an invented full date.</p>
</section>
