<section id="what-the-numbers-actually-say" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="what-the-numbers-actually-say">What the numbers actually say</h2>
<p><strong>Easy, well-bounded fields work.</strong> Governing law and agreement date sit at 0.9+ on both axes. They have one canonical answer and a predictable place in the document.</p>
<p><strong>The boolean fields are precise but timid.</strong> A precision of 1.00 with recall of 0.22 on liability caps means: when the model says “yes”, it’s right, but it says “yes” far too rarely. That is my own prompt’s fault. It tells the model to answer <code>False</code> unless a clause is explicit, so it misses most caps and non-competes. Nothing in a single accuracy number would have shown this. Splitting into precision and recall did.</p>
<p><strong>Parties are a labelling mismatch as much as a model problem.</strong> CUAD labels aliases and roles as separate spans, while the model returns one name per party, so recall tops out near 0.48.</p>
<p><strong><code>renewal_term</code> fails because it is free text.</strong> Matching loose prose to loose prose scores poorly whichever side is wrong. The fix is a better schema, not a better model.</p>
</section>
<section id="whats-next" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="whats-next">What’s next</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong><code>run_v2</code></strong>: relax the boolean prompt and re-score. Because runs are keyed by prompt hash, the comparison is one SQL query.</li>
<li><strong>Structured renewal terms</strong>: auto-renews, term length, notice period.</li>
<li><strong>Chunking</strong> for very long contracts, which are currently sent whole.</li>
<li><strong>Model comparison</strong>: the harness already keys runs by model, so swapping one in is cheap.</li>
</ul>
</section>
